#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fb3e6b9-359d-527e-b381-b4210d9e007d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291731} {:answer/id #uuid "a561a950-d397-503f-92e6-09e6900ad861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291732} {:answer/id #uuid "3eeafe43-e719-50d8-85c5-4b97ae46b68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291733} {:answer/id #uuid "a49f572c-a2e7-5ad0-82cc-bbac19d02802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291734} {:answer/id #uuid "776498a3-8e67-5302-b303-ef2cc1d2f1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3eeafe43-e719-50d8-85c5-4b97ae46b68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291733} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d4aa951-af9b-5825-9a3d-c4e6a9a0a712" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6c3a048-1e47-59de-8a45-c489c544f459" :question/math-academy-id "q-49546" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\, & ={\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/25)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/25}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/5)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/5)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (5\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89246796-f084-535b-9bcf-74cb453b66b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(64x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291737} {:answer/id #uuid "71ac8127-e14e-56c4-a515-c5897606da56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291738} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5e0987-5dd6-58f6-afd4-e850f1bd4974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291739} {:answer/id #uuid "a256eb39-0a5c-5bdf-b751-4a56f59a8ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(4x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291740} {:answer/id #uuid "a42e9ab2-f8e1-5790-8b1e-01e07d816281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(8x)}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a5e0987-5dd6-58f6-afd4-e850f1bd4974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291739} :answer-field/id #uuid "b69123c2-7d5f-5bb8-933f-37f2977e820c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c758c6a-6697-5dfb-b64a-bc653c6c226b" :question/math-academy-id "q-69476" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{8\\,\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{8\\,\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}=8\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $8x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=8x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}8\\int \\frac{1}{|8x|\\sqrt{(8x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =8\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(8x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd4861b-8cb0-523d-bb3c-5e1ce1d22a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(5x)}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291744} {:answer/id #uuid "b22db8de-8bc0-51eb-a8f8-01c6a1c13c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291745} {:answer/id #uuid "adb69dc9-6195-56ff-b839-818aede78a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291746} {:answer/id #uuid "2e971d87-82dc-5ce6-b986-b95dcbe2c313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arctan}(5x)}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291747} {:answer/id #uuid "27341bc8-8f58-540c-aa5e-78dde30499c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arctan}(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dd4861b-8cb0-523d-bb3c-5e1ce1d22a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(5x)}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291744} :answer-field/id #uuid "d56cc81d-323e-50ef-89fd-350113ffd0ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0bbddd4b-1bd5-542e-9d15-dd3b35f6f40f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73718" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{5|x|\\sqrt{25{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{5|x|\\sqrt{25{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $5x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{5|x|\\sqrt{25{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(5x)}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
